    "perplexity": {
      "description": "The album Dazed and Confused (Music From Motion Picture), released on September 28, 1993, by The Medicine Label, is a rock-focused soundtrack that captures the spirit of the mid-1970s through a curated selection of hard rock and classic rock tracks. It serves as the musical backdrop for Richard Linklater’s coming-of-age film Dazed and Confused, which depicts the last day of high school in 1976 Texas. The soundtrack features iconic rock anthems from artists such as Alice Cooper, Foghat, Rick Derringer, and ZZ Top, reflecting the era’s rebellious and energetic rock scene.\n\nWhile the album does not include every song heard in the film—some tracks like Bob Dylan’s “Hurricane,” Ted Nugent’s “Hey Baby,” and Aerosmith’s “Sweet Emotion” were excluded due to licensing costs—it remains a definitive collection that complements the film’s nostalgic tone. The soundtrack is notable for its authentic representation of 1970s rock and its role in enhancing the film’s cult status. Critically, the album was well received for its faithful evocation of the period’s musical landscape, contributing to the film’s enduring popularity among fans of classic rock and coming-of-age stories.",
